The Musgrave Ritual, works/20-the-musgrave-ritual.txt, lines 433–460 contains the ritual exchanges. The Hound of the Baskervilles, works/27-the-hound-of-the-baskervilles.txt, lines 1515–1539: newly bought brown boot stolen; lines 2108–2128: returned under cabinet during lunch, waiter denies knowledge; lines 7157–7172: new boot useless, returned, old obtained; lines 1874–1884: old black boot stolen; lines 6884–6901: old black boot found in mire, marked Meyers, Toronto; Stapleton used it to set hound on scent then threw it away while fleeing. lines 2132–2136 summarizes losses and return. The Crooked Man, works/22-the-crooked-man.txt, lines 63–79: Holmes deduces Watson's busy day from clean boots; says “Elementary” after “my dear Watson” in separate lines. Snopes, https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/elementary-my-dear-watson/ states not in Doyle canon, gives Crooked Man as closest; reports line in final scene of 1929 The Return of Sherlock Holmes and uncertainty about phrase origin. Accessed 2026-11-19.
